		"Blood: hits spray mist and droplets out the far side, spatter the floor or a wall behind the victim, bodies leave a spreading pool, and badly hurt players (under 30 HP) drip as they move. Clears each round; can be turned off in Settings > Display.",
		"Players are now detailed 3D soldiers instead of circles, modelled in Blender: CTs in plate carriers, camo-covered helmets with NVG mounts and knee pads; Ts in balaclavas, orange shemaghs, chest rigs and big packs with bedrolls. Two looks per side (CT cap and headset, T head wrap), and every player keeps theirs all match. Rifles are properly shouldered with both hands on the gun, pistols held out in two hands. A team-coloured ring underneath keeps sides readable at a glance.",
		"Grenades are 3D models too: a ribbed frag with spoon and pin, a vented flashbang, a smoke canister, the T molotov (a bottle with a rag) and the CT incendiary canister. You see the grenade in your hand and it tumbles through the air when thrown; the HUD and buy menu show them.",
		"Realistic explosions, smoke and fire, rendered from Blender smoke and fire simulations: frags and the bomb burst into a fireball with sparks and a flash of light, roll into black smoke and leave a scorch mark; smoke grenades build a thick, slowly turning cloud; molotovs burn as real flickering flames that light up their surroundings and char the ground.",
		"Match replays and killcams now show the soldiers, guns, C4, smoke and fire exactly like live play.",
		"Smarter bots when holding a contact. Instead of freezing wherever they were with the crosshair on the wall the enemy disappeared behind, a bot now works out the way the enemy would come at it and where they would appear. It moves to a nearby spot that sees that point at a good range for its gun (rifles and AWPs back off, shotguns sit on the corner), hidden from where the enemy is now and outside the angle the enemy will pre-aim, with cover a step away, and it spreads out from teammates into a crossfire. Its crosshair sits on the edge the enemy appears from, and it waits about as long as they would take to get there (hearing them again on the same route makes it wait longer instead of starting over). When they walk in it stands and shoots instead of strafing, and it reloads in cover, not on the angle. In 300 test duels against a peeker, holders now win 94 to 73 (the old hold was a coin flip, 79 to 81), have the crosshair on the enemy 92% of the time instead of 63%, and reload on a live angle far less often.",
		"Harbor, Foundry and Cage are rendered 3D maps now, like Dustline. Harbor is a container port on the water with moored cargo ships and a tug, warehouses with corrugated roofs, and shipping containers and cable drums for cover. Foundry is a steelworks with steel-plate bomb sites, factory halls with pipes and smokestacks, and machinery and steel crates for cover. Cage is a concrete training yard with jersey barriers and sandbags. Layouts and cover are unchanged.",
		"Every gun is now a real 3D model (all 21, modelled in Blender): players hold a top-down render of their actual gun, dropped guns lie on the floor showing their side profile, the HUD shows a picture of the gun in your hands, the buy menu has silhouettes on each row plus a full picture of the gun you hover, and the kill feed shows the killer's gun as a silhouette instead of its name. The bomb carrier wears the C4 on their back.",
		"The planted bomb is now a proper C4: six taped charges with a keypad, timer screen, detonators and wires, modelled in Blender and rendered top-down. The red light blinks on the timer itself. A dropped bomb uses the same model.",
		"Dustline has new 3D-rendered art: modelled and lit in Blender (walls with height and shadows, rooftops with AC units and water tanks, cars, barrels, crates, striped awnings, paved bomb sites with painted letters) and lined up exactly with the map grid.",
		"My Player: post to your feed. Type anything and pick a tone (hype the team, call out a rival, flame a teammate, blame the coach, fire back at the haters, apologise or neutral). The game reads what you wrote, names, insults, praise and apologies included, and works out who it's aimed at, even if that's not the tone you picked.",
		"Posts have consequences: followers, how your teammates feel about you, the coach's trust, club fines, sponsors dropping you over a toxic post, and rivals getting fired up for your next meeting. Fans, haters, the press, clubs and the people you mention reply in their own words. Three posts a week.",
		"My Player: a new Messages page. Message any player (teammates, rivals, free agents, players at other clubs), your team's group chat and the coach. Write freely: players answer in their own voice depending on their personality, mood, how they feel about you and what's been happening.",
		"Messages matter: tell a free agent or another club's player you want them and they may join your club or push for a move; ask a player about their club and it may come in for you; praise, apologies, banter and insults change how teammates feel about you; the coach can promise you starts, or hold a grudge. Players also message you first after big games, when you're benched and after rivalry games.",
		"Friends and rivals: players remember you. Time as teammates, friendly messages and supportive posts make friends; trash talk, insults and head-to-head battles make rivals, and ex-teammates remember how you left. Shown on the Messages page and in your Profile.",
		"More ways to lose your place: bad form, toxic posts, flaming teammates, falling out with the coach, sulking, demanding a transfer, the club signing a better player in your role, or budget cuts. You get a warning first, then a disciplinary bench, then the club can release you mid-season.",
		"Released? You're a free agent with no club, and there are ways back: play weekly showmatches (playable or simulated) to impress scouts, accept trial invites and win a contract in the practice match, let your agent shop you around (lower leagues first, better clubs as your reputation recovers), and answer stand-in calls from teams with an injured player. Your recent behaviour decides who calls.",
		"My Player: ask the coach for a start and he weighs your case: your last few ratings and form against the player starting ahead of you, your training this week, his trust in you and how you ask. A good, polite case can get you in the five even if he doesn't fully trust you yet; demands and insults backfire. He might also say \"keep this up this week and you start next week\", and keeps his word.",
		"Every club's coach has a personality (players' coach, disciplinarian or tactician) that changes what convinces him and how he talks.",
		"Conversations escalate: flame your teammates in the team chat or insult the coach and teammates go to the coach about you, ending in a warning, a disciplinary bench and finally release. Tell the coach you want to leave and the club transfer-lists you (real offers follow), terminates your contract if it doesn't rate you, or refuses if you're a key player with a long contract.",
		"Talking to other clubs' players leads somewhere: ask to join and they'll mention you to their coach, who messages you the next week with a contract offer, a pre-agreement that turns into an offer when the window opens, a trial, or a polite no (not good enough yet, your reputation, they just signed someone). Clubs you have beef with shut you down.",
		"How clubs see you (Career page): every club is interested, neutral, wary or won't sign you, based on your trash talk (posts and DMs), hate speech, toxic reputation, beef, how you left clubs and friends you have there. Clubs that won't sign you never make offers; wary ones rarely do.",
		"Trash talk in DMs carries into matches: confident players get fired up for your next meeting, shy ones get rattled, and if your messages leak their whole team is fired up and the club thinks less of you.",
		"Optional local AI chat: turn on Settings > Local AI chat (Ollama) to have a model running on your own PC word the replies in Messages and on your feed. The game still decides what every message does; if Ollama isn't running, the built-in replies are used.",
			"Knife (3): an original 3D combat knife you hold out in front, with a swipe animation and its own sounds. A hit in front does 40 damage, a backstab does 150 (a kill even through armour), you run fastest with it out, and a knife kill pays $1500. Shown in the HUD, kill feed and replays.",
		"Switching weapons now takes a second: guns, your pistol, the knife and grenades all have to be brought up before you can shoot, slash or throw (the HUD says DRAWING). Bots pay the same price, including after throwing a grenade.",
		"Your own eyes only: you no longer see what your teammates see. A new radar in the top left shows you, your teammates and enemies only where your team calls them out (markers that fade; a heard enemy is a rough ring).",
		"No more seeing hits through smoke or walls: hitting someone you can't see shows no red puff, blood spray or body.",
		"Smokes: a frag blows a big hole in a smoke for a few seconds, and spraying through one punches a lane that fills back in. Bots spray into a smoke when they know someone is behind it, and never hold from inside one.",
		"Flashbangs work from much further away (1100 px) with a flatter falloff, side-on still hurts, and turning away is your defence. Bots now spot or hear a flash coming and turn away from it, the better players more often.",
		"The bomb's blast radius is 50% bigger. Saving bots now find a real hiding spot, tucked into a corner well clear of the blast and out of the enemy's sight, instead of stopping just past the edge.",
		"Bots reload safely: only when they're hidden from the enemies they know about, otherwise in cover; the random reload on a live angle is much rarer and depends on skill. They switch to the pistol when that's faster than reloading with someone on them, AWPers pull the pistol when rushed, and the knife is a last resort.",
		"Less predictable T sides: mid is no longer the way in for most of the team every round. Each round sends 0 to 3 players through mid (usually 1 or 2), and teams avoid it after losing players there.",
		"Livelier all-chat, and personal: in a My Player career, rivals taunt you when they kill you, get salty when you kill them and call you out before rounds, rival teams talk trash, and friends on the other side are friendly. Show matches and trial matches are labelled as such.",
		"Slurs are hate speech, not banter: a post with one is removed (shown censored), the internet turns on you (followers lost, condemnation, the press), every sponsor walks, your club fines and suspends you for four weeks, and a repeat gets your contract terminated. In a DM the player blocks you and it often leaks. Common misspellings and leetspeak are caught.",
		"Replies to your posts now come in over the next minute or two instead of all at once, and likes climb from zero.",
		"Fix: shooting through a smoke grenade could tank the frame rate (smoke sight checks are about 10x cheaper).",
		"Footsteps carry half as far (for you and the bots).",
		"The bomb's defuse rattle is 60% quieter.",
		"Pathfinder is more accurate: a tighter cone, much less penalty for not scoping (2.5 instead of 6 degrees) or for moving.",
		"Messages feel like real conversations: with Local AI chat on, players and the coach see the whole recent conversation (and in the team chat, what teammates said), answer what you actually wrote, follow the topic, ask things back and react to each other. The game still decides what a message does.",
		"Replies take time: players read your message, then you see them typing, and the reply lands a few seconds later (teammates answer one after another in the group chat; the coach is slower).",
		"Feed posts have no tone buttons any more: the game reads what you wrote (hype, a call-out, flaming a teammate, a dig at the coach, an apology, firing back at haters) and who you named, and tells you how it was read.",
		"Camera: you stay in the middle of the screen, even at the edge of the map (it no longer stops at the border, so corners and edge spots are easy to see). It no longer drifts toward your cursor; hold Shift to look ahead.",
		"Standing in a smoke clouds your screen in grey: you see yourself and only a little around you (a frag or bullet hole clears it).",
		"Titles: trophies and awards earn titles like \"Premier League MVP (Season 2)\" or \"Challenger Cup Champion (Season 1)\". Wear one from your Profile: it shows under your name at the top of the hub and next to your name on the in-game scoreboard (Tab). Every player earns them too (whole teams for trophies, individuals for awards) and wears their best one. Existing careers get yours from the trophy cabinet.",
		"Homes you can walk around (My Player): a new Home page and a Go home button take you to your place, a top-down 3D-rendered home you walk with WASD. Press E at furniture to use it: sleep off fatigue (better homes restore more), aim practice or a stream on your PC (the desk upgrades with your setup level), watch pro demos, cook (better with a chef), work out, drive your car, play the piano or the arcade machine, soak in the hot tub, pet your dog or cat. Most count once a week. Your trophy cabinet shows your cups and lets you wear a title; your wardrobe has your outfits and weapon finishes.",
		"Nine places to live: a rented room to start, then three apartments (City apartment, Industrial loft, Seaside flat), three houses (City townhouse, Family house, Modern villa) and two penthouses (Skyline penthouse, Sky duplex). Each has its own layout and look. Tour any of them before buying; buying a better one moves you in, and you can move between the places you own.",
		"The Shop (new hub page) replaces the Lifestyle card: property, cars (hatchback, sports car, electric SUV, supercar, parked in your garage), home extras (streaming studio, chef, trophy cabinet, home gym, hot tub, arcade cabinet, aquarium, grand piano), pets (they roam your home), style (watch, gold chain, designer wardrobe, charity foundation), weapon finishes that colour your guns in matches (crimson, arctic, jungle, neon, gold) and personal staff (trainer +15% training, sports psychologist, personal physio).",
		"Investments: an index fund, crypto, an esports bar stake, an energy drink brand and an esports org stake. Values move every week, some pay a weekly share, and you can lose money.",
		"Your phone at home (P): invite a teammate over (they hang out at your place and you get closer), throw a house party (relationships and followers up, but the videos sometimes leak and the coach isn't happy) or book a vacation (fatigue gone, morale up; mid-season the coach notices).",
